<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sitting Bull</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1831–<a href="/years/1890">1890</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Tatanka Iyotake</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sitting Bull was a Hunkpapa Lakota holy man and leader who united Lakota and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cheyenne">Cheyenne</a> bands against American expansion onto the Great Plains. His followers defeated Custer at the Little Bighorn in 1876. After years in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a> he surrendered in 1881, and he was killed by Indian police during an attempt to arrest him in 1890.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sitting-bull.jpg" alt="Sitting Bull" width="320" height="441" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: David F. Barry, Photographer, Bismarck, Dakota Territory · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sitting_Bull_by_D_F_Barry_ca_1883_Dakota_Territory.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…an orthography in her fieldwork of the 1930s that anticipated much of the Standard Lakota Orthography, now the basis of the New Lakota Dictionary, with some 43,000 entries, and taught at <b>Sitting Bull</b> College, the Oglala Lakota County School District and Maȟpíya Lúta Owáyawa, formerly Red Cloud School.</blockquote><a href="/culture/lakota-people">Lakota people · Arts &amp; Knowledge</a></li><li><blockquote>The agents of the Bureau of Indian Affairs took fright; James McLaughlin called for troops and named <b>Sitting Bull</b> as the movement's leader, while the former agent Valentine McGillycuddy saw nothing out of the ordinary in the dances and warned that the troops themselves would bring trouble.</blockquote><a href="/songs/ghost-dance">Ghost Dance songs</a></li><li><blockquote>Lakota leader <b>Sitting Bull</b>, a leader at the victory over Custer at the Little Bighorn, was shot dead by Indian police trying to arrest him.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-15">15 December in history</a></li><li><blockquote>After gold was found in the Black Hills in 1874, the Great Sioux War followed; Lakota and Cheyenne under <b>Sitting Bull</b> and Crazy Horse defeated Custer at the Little Bighorn in 1876.</blockquote><a href="/state/great-sioux-nation">Great Sioux Nation</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Grand River. Died in Dakota Territory, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=46,-104&amp;year=1869" aria-label="Dakota Territory on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2092 544 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Sitting Bull"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2542 814h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2542 814h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=46,-104&amp;year=1869">What was here in 1869? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
